==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=Epistolary}}

===Etymology===
1650s, from {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|épistolaire}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} [[*epistolarium]], from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|epistola||letter}} (English {{m|en|epistle}}) + {{m|la|-arium}},<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref> from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|ἐπιστολή}} from {{m|grc|ἐπιστέλλω||I send a message}} from {{m|grc|ἐπί||upon}} + {{m|grc|στέλλω||I prepare, send}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{hyphenation|e|pis|to|la|ry|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ɪˈpɪst(ə)ˌləri/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|epistolaries}}

# a Christian liturgical book containing set readings for church services from the [[New Testament]] [[Epistles]].

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# of or relating to [[letter]]s, or the [[writing]] of letters
# carried on by [[written]] [[correspondence]]
# in the manner of written correspondence
#: '''''epistolary''' style''
#: ''an '''epistolary''' novel''

